WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.331
DAST findings need owners, remediation guidance, and validation.

2
00:00:04.331 --> 00:00:08.815
The fix should address the root cause, not merely silence the scanner.

3
00:00:08.815 --> 00:00:12.614
A security header gap may require platform configuration.

4
00:00:12.614 --> 00:00:17.022
An access control issue may require server-side permission checks.

5
00:00:17.022 --> 00:00:23.101
A session problem may require changes to cookie settings, token handling, or logout behavior.

6
00:00:23.101 --> 00:00:27.584
Good guidance explains expected secure behavior and how to verify it.

7
00:00:27.584 --> 00:00:35.259
Retesting after remediation confirms that the issue was actually fixed and that runtime behavior changed as expected.

8
00:00:35.259 --> 00:00:45.214
Retesting should use the same scope and comparable conditions when practical, but it should also consider whether the fix created new behavior elsewhere.

9
00:00:45.214 --> 00:00:54.485
Security regression testing can help prevent recurring issues by adding checks to automated tests, release criteria, or future scan profiles.

10
00:00:54.485 --> 00:01:00.184
DAST can be integrated into CI/CD and release decisions, but it should be done carefully.

11
00:01:00.184 --> 00:01:07.859
Scheduled scans, baseline scans, pre-release scans, and environment-specific scans each serve different purposes.

12
00:01:07.859 --> 00:01:20.321
Release gates and severity thresholds can help prevent high-risk issues from shipping, but noisy unreviewed gates can train teams to ignore results or seek exceptions without fixing problems.

13
00:01:20.321 --> 00:01:25.869
Exception workflows and risk acceptance should be documented, owned, and revisited.

14
00:01:25.869 --> 00:01:35.747
Tracking unresolved findings, aging, validation status, and recurring root causes turns DAST into an improvement program rather than a report factory.

15
00:01:35.747 --> 00:01:45.702
The goal is developer workflow support: timely evidence, practical remediation, and risk-informed release decisions without unnecessary disruption.
